<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balkan Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stara Planina, Haemus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balkan Mountains are a range running about 550 kilometres east to west across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, known in antiquity as the Haemus. Their passes, such as the Shipka Pass, controlled routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> plain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, making them a frontier and defensive line for Byzantine and Bulgarian rulers. The range gave its name to the whole Balkan Peninsula.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains.jpg" alt="Balkan Mountains" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ikonact, RosarioVanTulpe, Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MontesBalcanes_(en).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
